('10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','Light: Reflection and Refraction','Physics','Class 10','Ray optics from plane and spherical mirrors to refraction, refractive index and lenses. A complete, prerequisite-led Class 10 course.','published')
insert into public.concepts(id,course_id,slug,title,summary,order_index,content) values
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','laws-of-reflection','Laws of reflection','How light bounces from a surface and how a plane mirror forms an image.',0,$c${"explanation":"When light strikes a smooth boundary it reflects so that the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, both measured from the normal at the point of incidence. The incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in the same plane. A plane mirror forms a virtual, upright image that is the same size as the object and as far behind the mirror as the object is in front. The image is laterally inverted and cannot be caught on a screen. Rough surfaces reflect diffusely, scattering light so objects are visible from many directions.","examples":[{"problem":"A person stands 2 m in front of a plane mirror. How far behind the mirror is the image, and how far is it from the person?","solution":"The image is 2 m behind the mirror, so it is 4 m from the person."},{"problem":"A ray strikes a plane mirror at 30° to the normal. Find the angle between the incident and reflected rays.","solution":"The reflected ray is also 30° from the normal on the other side, so the angle between the rays is 60°."}],"misconceptions":["The image lies on the mirror surface.","The angle of incidence is measured from the mirror surface rather than the normal.","A plane mirror physically swaps a person's left and right sides."],"revision_notes":"i = r, measured from the normal. Plane mirror: virtual, upright, same size, equal distance behind the mirror, laterally inverted."}$c$::jsonb),
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','spherical-mirrors','Spherical mirrors','Concave and convex mirrors, their terms and ray diagrams.',1,$c${"explanation":"A spherical mirror is part of a hollow sphere. The pole P is its centre on the mirror, C is the centre of curvature and F is the principal focus. For a mirror of small aperture the focal length is half the radius of curvature: f = R/2. A concave mirror converges parallel rays and can form real, inverted images; a convex mirror diverges parallel rays and always forms a virtual, upright, diminished image. Rays parallel to the principal axis pass through F after reflection from a concave mirror and appear to come from F behind a convex mirror.","examples":[{"problem":"A concave mirror has radius of curvature 24 cm. Find its focal length.","solution":"f = R/2 = 24/2 = 12 cm, in front of the mirror."},{"problem":"Where is the image of a distant object formed by a concave mirror?","solution":"Parallel rays from a distant object meet at the principal focus, so the image forms at F."}],"misconceptions":["The focus is the same point as the centre of curvature.","A convex mirror can form a real image on a screen.","Focal length equals radius of curvature."],"revision_notes":"Concave converges, convex diverges. f = R/2. A convex mirror always gives a virtual, erect, diminished image."}$c$::jsonb),
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000103','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','mirror-formula','Mirror formula and magnification','Relating object distance, image distance and focal length with signs.',2,$c${"explanation":"For spherical mirrors, 1/v + 1/u = 1/f, where distances are measured from the pole. Using the usual sign convention, the object distance u is negative for a real object, f is negative for a concave mirror and positive for a convex mirror. Magnification is m = -v/u: a negative m means a real inverted image, while a positive m means a virtual upright image; |m| greater than 1 means enlarged. A virtual image is formed where reflected rays only appear to meet, so it cannot be projected on a screen.","examples":[{"problem":"An object is 15 cm in front of a concave mirror of focal length 10 cm. Find the image distance and magnification.","solution":"With u = -15 cm and f = -10 cm: 1/v = 1/f - 1/u = -1/10 + 1/15 = -1/30, so v = -30 cm. m = -v/u = -(-30)/(-15) = -2: real, inverted, twice as large."},{"problem":"An object is 30 cm in front of a convex mirror of focal length 15 cm. Find v and m.","solution":"u = -30 cm, f = +15 cm: 1/v = 1/15 + 1/30 = 1/10, so v = +10 cm. m = -v/u = 1/3: virtual, upright, diminished."}],"misconceptions":["Object distance is positive for a real object under the sign convention.","Magnification sign has no meaning.","A virtual image means light does not reach the observer."],"revision_notes":"1/v + 1/u = 1/f and m = -v/u, all distances from the pole. Negative m: real and inverted. Positive m: virtual and upright."}$c$::jsonb),
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000104','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','refraction','Refraction of light','Why light bends at a boundary and how Snell’s law predicts it.',3,$c${"explanation":"Light changes speed when it passes from one transparent medium to another, and this change of speed bends its path: this is refraction. Going from a rarer to a denser medium, light slows down and bends toward the normal; going from denser to rarer it speeds up and bends away from the normal. Snell’s law is n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r. A ray striking the boundary along the normal (i = 0) passes straight through without bending. Refraction explains why a pencil appears bent in water and why a pool looks shallower than it is.","examples":[{"problem":"Light passes from air (n = 1.00) into glass (n = 1.50) at 30° to the normal. Find the angle of refraction.","solution":"sin r = (n₁/n₂) sin i = (1.00/1.50)(0.5) = 0.333, so r ≈ 19.5°, smaller than 30° because the ray bends toward the normal."},{"problem":"Does light bend when it enters water exactly along the normal?","solution":"No. i = 0 gives r = 0; the direction is unchanged although the speed changes."}],"misconceptions":["Light bends at every boundary regardless of the angle of incidence.","Frequency changes when light enters a new medium.","Light bends because it is pushed by the new medium rather than because its speed changes."],"revision_notes":"Slower medium: ray bends toward the normal. n₁ sin i = n₂ sin r. No bending at normal incidence, but speed still changes."}$c$::jsonb),
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','refractive-index','Refractive index','Comparing light speed in a medium with its speed in vacuum.',4,$c${"explanation":"The absolute refractive index of a medium is n = c/v, where c is the speed of light in vacuum and v is its speed in the medium. Because v is never greater than c, n is at least 1. A larger refractive index means the medium is optically denser and light travels more slowly in it. For two media, the relative refractive index is n₂₁ = v₁/v₂ = n₂/n₁. Refractive index is a property of the material and the wavelength of light; it has no unit.","examples":[{"problem":"Light travels at 2.0 × 10⁸ m/s in a medium. Find its refractive index.","solution":"n = c/v = (3.0 × 10⁸)/(2.0 × 10⁸) = 1.5."},{"problem":"Water has n = 1.33 and glass n = 1.50. In which does light travel faster?","solution":"In water, because a smaller refractive index means a larger speed: v = c/n."}],"misconceptions":["A higher refractive index means light travels faster.","Refractive index has units of metres per second.","Optical density is the same as mass density."],"revision_notes":"n = c/v, unitless, n ≥ 1. Larger n means slower light. Relative index n₂₁ = n₂/n₁."}$c$::jsonb),
('20000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106','10000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','lenses','Lenses and lens formula','Convex and concave lenses, image formation and lens power.',5,$c${"explanation":"A convex lens is thicker at the centre and converges parallel rays to its principal focus; a concave lens is thinner at the centre and diverges them. For a thin lens, 1/v - 1/u = 1/f and magnification m = v/u, with distances measured from the optical centre using the sign convention. Lens power is P = 1/f with f in metres, measured in dioptres (D); a converging lens has positive power and a diverging lens negative power. A convex lens can form real or virtual images depending on object distance, while a concave lens always forms a virtual, upright, diminished image.","examples":[{"problem":"An object is 15 cm from a convex lens of focal length 10 cm. Find the image distance and magnification.","solution":"u = -15 cm, f = +10 cm: 1/v = 1/10 + 1/(-15) = 1/30, so v = +30 cm. m = v/u = -2: real, inverted, twice as large."},{"problem":"A lens has focal length 50 cm. Find its power.","solution":"f = 0.50 m, so P = 1/0.50 = +2.0 D, a converging lens."}],"misconceptions":["A convex lens always magnifies.","The lens formula uses the same signs as the mirror formula for every term.","Power is measured in metres."],"revision_notes":"Thin lens: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f, m = v/u. P = 1/f (f in metres), unit dioptre. Diverging lens: P negative."}$c$::jsonb)
